#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/id #uuid "ec1aaa71-efe4-50fd-8552-bcc98abbd3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-218" :question/problem "Each side of a square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}?$\n\n![](images/a0/a06eb0963dc6d01e45acb9349e5232ba70a11c98c99ee63c2ec0dae3d748f5ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} .\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min},$ so we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(-2)\\cdot 2(10)=-40{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at a rate of $40\\,\\text{cm}^2/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/id #uuid "8780fe5c-94c5-5ac6-b6f6-6e86df29b416" :question/math-academy-id "e-219" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $20 \\text{ cm}^2\\text{/s}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds." :question/worked-solution "This situation is different. Here, the *area* is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\text{ cm[math]^2[/math]/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "6264adc4-c5ab-5b84-b89f-27b4382baa0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-220" :question/problem "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{ mm}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "7187c402-9949-5671-af33-8929bc5344a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-221" :question/problem "The sides of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to twice the rate of increase of the diagonal. What is the area of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0843cafa-6c1b-588f-a4b6-400d353520a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499669} {:answer/id #uuid "1f35e7ed-cb3e-54ad-9e21-254f09984942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.05{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499725} {:answer/id #uuid "74367f1d-8b69-58fd-86eb-c4d6b8b5fc47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499868} {:answer/id #uuid "dd24220e-3876-55fa-81e4-4fd007c1ad14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.25{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500109} {:answer/id #uuid "eeeeed9f-a43d-552c-8c0e-e7e72a8e5855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35.2{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd24220e-3876-55fa-81e4-4fd007c1ad14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.25{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500109} :answer-field/id #uuid "f56de714-5cdc-5f19-b3b0-cef270a1eb6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dde4916-a480-580f-9e20-db5c7a8b1aba" :question/math-academy-id "q-115923" :question/problem "The sides (in inches) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to eleven times the rate of increase of the sides. What is the area of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=11\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 11\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 11 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=5.5\\text{ in}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={5.5}^{2}=30.25{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fad524f3-747b-543d-877c-19d0bc18ff69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371592} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d8801c-f0ce-5902-a2aa-2522226312ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371593} {:answer/id #uuid "5f70dac8-847a-5a3c-afa9-5b5ad69de949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371594} {:answer/id #uuid "2c00a4c0-f033-55e1-9d1b-40d157b2fcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371595} {:answer/id #uuid "e6aeaa69-cb2d-572c-ad4b-0228aa3f284a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1d8801c-f0ce-5902-a2aa-2522226312ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371593} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59fb865-44ed-5cc4-bf4d-8ff775b26354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "332ede8a-b7d8-56ae-b05d-98854e11eb9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115930" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $56{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square decreasing when the sides are $8\\text{ m}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/1c/1cb323f4c38bb4fc13ffc81f47544424872480b42f033032da47c04d097e8696.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-56\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-56 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{28}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $8\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=8.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{28}{8}=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf51f91-0a6d-569d-8c03-c177edaa1dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398139} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1ee6ea-b061-51dc-b501-839195b28399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398140} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4f5765-990d-5307-bcda-feb606bb929b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398141} {:answer/id #uuid "58cdd8af-ecc0-5a53-9201-682ba5b6106b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398142} {:answer/id #uuid "d97728d8-7183-59a2-bbab-433924b0cf9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58cdd8af-ecc0-5a53-9201-682ba5b6106b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398142} :answer-field/id #uuid "015670c7-de29-5d58-b9b4-055602c56ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c40d4d8-93cd-5fd9-9c9b-8b9eaffb8fe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-116051" :question/problem "![](images/30/30b101fa9812c49237ab72c0c4aebd1666df6de80367eb2ee59b33fe0e422d87.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-12\\text{ m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0798d16f-f5b7-5492-a8d2-0850252014fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499667} {:answer/id #uuid "131831ae-535d-565d-82c5-5eeef54c306a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499697} {:answer/id #uuid "6070339a-825a-5fe2-880f-267d7547184e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499842} {:answer/id #uuid "865d0e1a-3c0c-5af4-bcfa-841746f13cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499914} {:answer/id #uuid "bb402b80-0491-5b26-9210-7d2228540603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "131831ae-535d-565d-82c5-5eeef54c306a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499697} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8252af9-6724-51ce-b683-1b74d5d5cb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb99c2d9-3bf4-5523-9da0-10f469bef9ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-116052" :question/problem "![](images/88/88adbcd9d9bd93da5386546b517d765ef6ccd584ae690e7d4ce0d3047cdfc48c.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}\\text{/s},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-7\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-7\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-28\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $28\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89c12662-6dd9-59fc-9fe9-236a35f3e35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\text{ m}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499922} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8b9716-e2de-5c53-a065-b7d01beb7a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\text{ m}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499999} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5aa18a-f522-5524-af5f-96af2c3c7291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500040} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ac6247-20c9-56f4-95b4-54c90039906a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500065} {:answer/id #uuid "ded90f3d-f805-5c66-a067-ce35f5768a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd5aa18a-f522-5524-af5f-96af2c3c7291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500040} :answer-field/id #uuid "653606cc-002e-51b5-a80e-ba0f2d8215f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82bcc198-7907-5fda-ad7c-ebdd316cd930" :question/math-academy-id "q-2770" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc27a817900f1e7bef61f73da6bbdb3aaae4e30d59490dce4395416d98f4edae.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $2\\text{ m/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $10\\text{ m}$ long?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(2)\\cdot 2(10)=40{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f9369b-e6c7-5f61-8fc5-c873059be79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499814} {:answer/id #uuid "762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499872} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec08695-a478-5d1f-a7ad-7cc9d9bedfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499933} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf8fb62-6cae-52c3-b37d-05d7c31d9523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500076} {:answer/id #uuid "df357ea5-8c84-50a9-8a37-c8ef860e991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499872} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e91639-868e-5146-8866-35d968fb2a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "850324c7-bb98-55b1-99ad-faf0afddb108" :question/math-academy-id "q-2777" :question/problem "![](images/19/199f1c7e18eb0c25bf7caafb8e2e71fa3e0165af81083ea57c1fc16a74935ab3.png)\n\nA square's diagonals decrease at the rate of  $2\\text{ m}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square decreasing when the diagonals are $3\\text{ m}$ long each?" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=3.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 3=-6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f767d27-f5ed-5560-a033-3e945bc4cde9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f767d27-f5ed-5560-a033-3e945bc4cde9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499775} :answer-field/id #uuid "9865f5a2-d5d6-5e7c-9f42-56cea805a3bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b15db96-5f99-5a26-80eb-fe738443b083" :question/math-academy-id "q-314490" :question/problem "The sides (in meters) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to fourteen times the rate of increase of the sides. What is the area $A$ of the square at that moment?\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{ m}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 14\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 14 & =2x \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=7\\text{ m}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={7}^{2}=49{\\text{ m}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542} :answer-field/id #uuid "6262049f-56fe-5753-8d3a-24cb4987048f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d06153ec-06de-5e08-a5e0-36bc3ef9e7e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-314495" :question/problem "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to seven times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length $h$ of the diagonal of the square at that moment?\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 7\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 7 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $7\\,\\text{ ft}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "727566af-2b70-549e-be78-bf01a429c02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727566af-2b70-549e-be78-bf01a429c02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499865} :answer-field/id #uuid "99ac03b7-904c-5b89-9b95-3862238cdcb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcf0d4e1-9821-591f-a3fd-6de72f482ee3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344455" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1fd2786b6a375a4c1d0151b38ad5daf222631ee33e96bc02ada00ef67e051ce.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $6\\text{ m/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the side is $5\\,\\text{m}$ long?\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{m/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6.$ Also, when the sides are $5\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=5.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\cdot 2(5) \\\\  & =60{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at\n$60\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372901} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6ddcc7-548b-528c-b8e3-9ff38fb06abf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "175199e1-96a5-594b-b670-66eb928d5ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344507" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $30{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a4/a42b7ac5c9f5870d256b6474c9b95730d32184ba22ca80235c76ffe0ff346f31.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\text{ }{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{15}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c85fd9e-d734-57a2-b3b3-5f7387d158d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c85fd9e-d734-57a2-b3b3-5f7387d158d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499958} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea4e14ee-7928-5c6b-b973-9fa55f6b887b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8bf196f-ddea-5382-bd16-d659e05ea09d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344525" :question/problem "The area of a square is decreasing at a rate of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the side of ​​the square decreasing when the side is $4\\text{ cm}$ long?\n\nThe side is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/10/10e47419775a585e10e732f482ee279bba49d205ee12959cd80c8d33924accbf.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-48\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-48 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{48}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{48}{2\\cdot 4}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the side is decreasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a07a90e1-3f2d-527c-95e4-9e7865dfa008" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f2920eb-bbfa-5091-822d-b1cc3cc2b1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344607" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate is the diagonal of the square decreasing when the diagonal is $20\\text{ cm}$ long?\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-10.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $20\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $h=20.$ Substituting this information into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal is decreasing at $0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42afc9a4-d297-5d8a-b35b-dccdfacc0dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42afc9a4-d297-5d8a-b35b-dccdfacc0dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499781} :answer-field/id #uuid "a52d0c63-7cee-55f6-be50-18ed8125a84f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c26c64d2-f1a3-5c4d-8afc-7e98e275ec7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344610" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4851331d4899976fb59bb469f19604716fd33235470d24a725abbb26e886279.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square are decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate is the perimeter of the square decreasing?\n\nThe perimeter is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}\\,h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-7\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}=(-7\\sqrt{2})(2\\sqrt{2})=-28\\text{ m/min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $28\\,\\text{m/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1297eee-ae31-559a-8063-29c28074e2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1297eee-ae31-559a-8063-29c28074e2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372907} :answer-field/id #uuid "240a125a-7965-503b-98eb-0c6543b7dbff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d743fe47-5489-54f0-9f74-3b3ae11c1658" :question/math-academy-id "q-344614" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f440744e4dd65883ae2ae564595491b36c1371a553c2052d2923f8f617caaf2.png)\n\nA manufacturing process gradually reduces the size of a square opening by shortening its diagonals at a rate of $2.2\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the opening decreasing when each diagonal measures $10\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ be the length of the diagonals and $A$ be the area of the square. Using the Pythagorean theorem, the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.2\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2.2.\n$$\n\nWhen the diagonal length is $h=10,$ substituting into the expression above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=(-2.2)(10)=-22{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square opening is decreasing at a rate of $22{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500052} :answer-field/id #uuid "540ea305-c7f1-5022-950d-9653c1a23171" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" :question/math-academy-id "q-344664" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef685549b562c570914bc8b5b5066cb621e42c8536586b37467afc6c1eb6c194.png)\n\nAn adjustable square skylight is being expanded as part of a building renovation. The area of the skylight is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals increasing when each diagonal measures $18\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe diagonal is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square skylight, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=27.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=18.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 27 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =1.5\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the skylight is increasing at a rate of \n$1.5\\text{ mm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079398ab-7ac1-54ec-8313-d5341767d2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499666} {:answer/id #uuid "2456cc6c-5e4d-5920-b972-00d894004b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499735} {:answer/id #uuid "342916e8-5acb-52d6-a2fa-48b79d7d1f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499762} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0d9c47-25a9-5034-b11f-37b02fa593f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499924} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf7b751-e003-5879-bd97-6773f0dd0b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbf7b751-e003-5879-bd97-6773f0dd0b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500173} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d5917d-5bf7-5e58-a475-dcbda538b4bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7140d279-862c-5a41-be5a-097813177226" :question/math-academy-id "q-48338" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $25{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square increasing when the sides are $2\\text{ cm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a2/a2bd105cbf7aa6fc2af8b2189e63122acfbb0bcf70b007cd35efd8492d273dca.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =25\\text{ cm}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $2\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=2.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{25}{2\\cdot (2)}=\\frac{25}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are increasing at a rate of $\\frac{25}{4}\\,\\text{cm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8407c1-3175-5166-ab8e-c0f6c6813aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062924} {:answer/id #uuid "e1308e12-f501-5153-88f2-76e216435b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062925} {:answer/id #uuid "0456556b-527c-530f-bae6-45f5a5491b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062926} {:answer/id #uuid "368933d9-de3c-5973-9020-320c9eb737b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062927} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbe0d2f-31b8-54e9-b7b0-4f96a8068d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fbe0d2f-31b8-54e9-b7b0-4f96a8068d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062928} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e13f342-74ba-5f29-a5e3-5849701e2a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d6bf115-1a99-51dd-8a30-6ca9b66d4cb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48339" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $10{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/hour}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/d7/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\text{ in}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{5}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c33f2db-ee69-5899-b254-c9920d0cc08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398857} {:answer/id #uuid "07f94e7c-eaf0-50a5-82b2-936289c3244e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398858} {:answer/id #uuid "0b0dea4f-92e3-5582-a8c3-1096bf841a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398859} {:answer/id #uuid "df4e741b-904e-553c-b625-b7472668b725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398860} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5bdcf3-a541-5f6f-8726-df468ddf9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c5bdcf3-a541-5f6f-8726-df468ddf9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398861} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8c5ef9-0c37-5adf-839c-99af73ffe32d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "51c2bb08-d00d-5a09-b26c-2e5b5f8107fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48372" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f636c252da8e886c1f4bf9f0d87387339fce91e5a72f6275e6b24bba787d288.png)\n\nAnna is an environmentalist and is studying the impact of deforestation in a pine forest located in a square-shaped region. Anna discovers that the area of ​​the deforested region is decreasing at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month}.$ At what rate are the region's diagonals decreasing if the diagonals are $20\\text{ km}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=20.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{0.4}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.02\\,\\text{km}\\text{/month}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the region are decreasing at a rate of  $0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499693} {:answer/id #uuid "a772f7bf-13d7-5a11-b09f-eb30c2bf7b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499978} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8e0339-f5c6-51f4-989a-0cdb574502ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499994} {:answer/id #uuid "c44d2824-b8b1-5867-82fe-eb017c5a781d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500051} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3e88a9-534e-5c2c-95bc-3fa313944feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499693} :answer-field/id #uuid "a33fd242-f46e-5e85-8083-9fc0a177d7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fede564-db3c-5749-8f57-8bfacce22a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-48375" :question/problem "![](images/97/97a7d465f8412c88367ed3cf148af3ce387441284c10e3f84888c0186fa597ce.png)\n\nPeter wants to cut a piece of square-shaped glass while maintaining its shape. The area of this glass is decreasing at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate are the diagonals of the glass decreasing if the length of these diagonals is $50\\text{ in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=50.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot (-1.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1.5}{50} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the glass are decreasing at a rate of $0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "823f29bb-0f89-5de2-8494-842a1f5859f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499902} {:answer/id #uuid "99ef838f-ba04-56c6-8ad7-d24b062b203d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499953} {:answer/id #uuid "ab62f859-7f30-514a-8597-c0b385aa0a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499992} {:answer/id #uuid "b0487d7e-a1c5-586f-87a9-4ac684e34472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500006} {:answer/id #uuid "c32beff5-1b79-5499-b695-6642a4a93125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ef838f-ba04-56c6-8ad7-d24b062b203d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499953} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae71706-c0f4-5bda-827d-bb7d8c83ba6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17fd30dd-fe66-5909-8dae-293b86cae983" :question/math-academy-id "q-73309" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6462df728eefef5a942fb409153dc1ba67d4bc065afb281f86b1bd9ac751d20.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $7\\,\\text{mm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $7\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=7.$ Also, when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $x=12.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(7)\\cdot 2(12)=168{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $168\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$"}]}
